target=\"_blank\">Mage</a> | <a href=\"https://goo.gl/2HCpjg\" target=\"_blank\">Mummy</a> | <a href=\"https://goo.gl/68yRHY\" target=\"_blank\">Promethean</a> | <a href=\"https://goo.gl/a4mjqA\" target=\"_blank\">Vampire</a> | <a href=\"https://goo.gl/hrULtN\" target=\"_blank\">Werewolf</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4433-second-chances","name":"Second Chances","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4433_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4202"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-03-25T06:02:52-07:00","id":1441368,"title":"","content":"I all the time watch <b>John Oliver</b> on Youtube - his episode on Plastic below. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/cool.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Cool\" smilieid=\"6\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> <div class=\"videocontainer with-aspect-ratio videocontainer-horizontal\" style=\"aspect-ratio: 16/9;\"> <a class=\"video-frame\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Fiu9GSOmt8E\" data-code=\"Fiu9GSOmt8E\" data-provider=\"youtube\" data-height=\"0\" data-width=\"0\" itemprop=\"video\"> <div class=\"videothumbnail\" style=\"\"> <img src=\"https://img.youtube.com/vi/Fiu9GSOmt8E/maxresdefault.jpg\" alt=\"\" /> <div class=\"videothumbnailoverlay\"></div> </div> </a> </div>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1437-wyrdhamster","name":"wyrdhamster","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1437_5.jpg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11313"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-04-17T10:03:56-07:00","id":1444348,"title":"","content":"Why WE MUST have Nuclear Energy \u2622\ufe0f to stop Climate Change on Earth! \ud83c\udf0d ( <b>In a Nutshell</b> video analysis. )<br /> <br /> <div class=\"videocontainer with-aspect-ratio videocontainer-horizontal\" style=\"aspect-ratio: 16/9;\"> <a class=\"video-frame\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EhAemz1v7dQ\" data-code=\"EhAemz1v7dQ\" data-provider=\"youtube\" data-height=\"0\" data-width=\"0\" itemprop=\"video\"> <div class=\"videothumbnail\" style=\"\"> <img src=\"https://img.youtube.com/vi/EhAemz1v7dQ/maxresdefault.jpg\" alt=\"\" /> <div class=\"videothumbnailoverlay\"></div> </div> </a> </div><br /> <br /> <img src=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EhAemz1v7dQ\" class=\"bbcode-attachment bbcode-attachment--lightbox js-lightbox\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" itemprop=\"image\" loading=\"lazy\"/>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1437-wyrdhamster","name":"wyrdhamster","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1437_5.jpg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11313"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-04-17T10:37:21-07:00","id":1444357,"title":"","content":"I think that video is rather more tempered for good reason.<br /> <br /> For those that don't watch it, the video makes the case that nuclear power is the only logical way to transition off of fossil fuels and reduce emissions in a timely fashion. I think it does a good job of that argument, but it is honest in that it isn't a &quot;must&quot; as much as &quot;the way that makes the most sense of our current options.&quot;<br /> <br /> For example, one of the reasons to argue for large investments in nuclear power is that energy demand keeps going up at a very high rate; a rate that is currently exceeding renewable energy's ability to scale up. The rate in which renewable energy sources are being improved, installed/replaced, and expanded to consumers isn't matching the rate of increased demand. We could put lots and lots (and lots) more money into that conflict (both sides even; reducing demand and increasing renewable output) but there's a limit to how effective that is and we don't really know if we can do that and meet the projected needs to keep climate change in check. Modern nuclear plants, in terms of practical cost, can be installed with that investment instead for a technology we know will result a massive drop in emissions at scale, and without having to solve electrical grid issues like storing and releasing renewable energy efficiently to create a constant supply out of variable sources. There's also a bunch of very promising nuclear power technologies that are already proven on small scale, that could use a major investment for scaling up and reduce the negatives of nuclear further.<br /> <br /> So even if the end goal is to get to as much renewable power as possible, nuclear is the most rational bridge from now to then taking as many factors as possible into account.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-04-26T05:24:01-07:00","id":1445961,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1397961-why-is-climate-change-so-controversial?p=1444357#post1444357\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think that video is rather more tempered for good reason.<br /> <br /> For those that don't watch it, the video makes the case that nuclear power is the only logical way to transition off of fossil fuels and reduce emissions in a timely fashion. I think it does a good job of that argument, but it is honest in that it isn't a &quot;must&quot; as much as &quot;the way that makes the most sense of our current options.&quot;<br /> <br /> For example, one of the reasons to argue for large investments in nuclear power is that energy demand keeps going up at a very high rate; a rate that is currently exceeding renewable energy's ability to scale up. The rate in which renewable energy sources are being improved, installed/replaced, and expanded to consumers isn't matching the rate of increased demand. We could put lots and lots (and lots) more money into that conflict (both sides even; reducing demand and increasing renewable output) but there's a limit to how effective that is and we don't really know if we can do that and meet the projected needs to keep climate change in check. Modern nuclear plants, in terms of practical cost, can be installed with that investment instead for a technology we know will result a massive drop in emissions at scale, and without having to solve electrical grid issues like storing and releasing renewable energy efficiently to create a constant supply out of variable sources. There's also a bunch of very promising nuclear power technologies that are already proven on small scale, that could use a major investment for scaling up and reduce the negatives of nuclear further.<br /> <br /> So even if the end goal is to get to as much renewable power as possible, nuclear is the most rational bridge from now to then taking as many factors as possible into account.</div> </div> </div> </div>agree with you buddy<br /> you say everything right","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26777-bernhigg","name":"BernhigG","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26777_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-04-26T14:43:58-07:00","id":1445994,"title":"","content":"Thank you, though I will note that was a lot more of me trying to summarize the video in depth more than inherently stating my position on the matter.<br /> <br /> While I understand the limitations of a 10-15 minute YouTube video that's just trying to make the basic facts clear for people, one of the things that I think isn't highlighted enough in these sorts of discussions is that we don't have to pick some sort of global one-size-fits-all plan. In fact, I'd argue we shouldn't.<br /> <br /> IMO the push for increased nuclear should be in the highly developed Western nations, and the heavily populated main-land Asian nations. The common factors here being relatively high energy demands, and existing &quot;old&quot; electric infrastructure. Weening North America, Europe, India, and China off of fossil fuels with nuclear makes sense for all the reasons that video laid out.<br /> <br /> But there's also good reasons why say, Japan, needs a different consideration. It's very hard to build more nuclear in Japan that isn't going to be at further increase risk of being hit hard by natural disaster as sea levels rise, storms get more powerful, and so on. Japan has a high population and high energy demand, but there's a strong case to let them have more of the carbon footprint while they transition more directly to renewables rather than trying to build more nuclear on the islands. A harder push for nuclear in the US and Canada would easily lessen the burden on nations with more significant practical issues like that.<br /> <br /> Then you look at lots of Africa, and they're not at the reliance on fossil fuels levels the more developed parts of the world are. They're starting to invest in fossil fuels more than ever, because it's fast, cheap,and they want to compete in the global market. This would be a great place to put less focus on nuclear, and more on building up a renewable based electrical system from the ground up while national and international scale power grids are being built up in the first place. But this would take active and substantial investment from the wealthier nations to make happen (though so would trying to get them to use nuclear)..<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-14T18:58:57-07:00","id":1448556,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://youtu.be/HnJ4gjVZqao\" target=\"_blank\">Second Thought did a pretty good video</a> about water shortages.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>He/him","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/177-thecountalucard","name":"TheCountAlucard","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar177_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10857"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-14T22:51:13-07:00","id":1448569,"title":"","content":"The general information on things like why water wars are coming, and how inefficient many of our current systems are is good, but the (however much cynicism is justified) on how the US and the rest of the neo-liberal democratic capitalist countries will use imperial military force to make everything worse really doesn't actually help anyone know what to do about it... since we need countries like the US to fix their problems. Fresh water is not actually a simple commodity to transport long distances, so the idea that we'll intervene in fights in, say, central Africa and then siphon off water for ourselves is rather overblown.<br /> <br /> And Bill Gates doesn't need anyone licking his shoes, but one of the good things his charity actually works on is increasing water use efficiency.... so I'm pretty sure there's lots of super rich people to attack as assholes that aren't at least trying to toss money at the problem.<br /> <br /> So, water is going to get so scarce people are going to fight (more) over it. The US is an exemplar of bad water use culture. Rich assholes aren't going to save us (even if Bill Gates funds lots of research into these things, that's not at all enough for the globe). <br /> <br /> Even if you had the power to snap your fingers and completely reorganize the five top water wasters in the world to your ideal governmental systems, and they passed water saving measures ASAP, it's not going to fix the problem, just delay it. It's also not going to stop small regional fights. Since it's in the news... what would you do about the already extant water crisis in the Gaza Strip? All of Gaza's water comes from Israeli water pipes, with a bit of humanitarian aid (but that's not enough to cover drinking water, let alone growing crops or basic sanitation and hygiene). You think the Israelis aren't going to take the first chance they can to cut off that water supply (it's already in drought conditions since the same water source is already also going to the West Bank, Jordan, Syria, and Lebanon) so they can keep the levels they say they need?<br /> <br /> It's great to label some bad people, and bad ideology, but it doesn't inform people about what they could actually be doing to help stop this. Keep in mind that even things like &quot;go vegan, look at how much water we waste on cows!&quot; runs into the point about how much we use water intensive crops for direct consumption as well. Cow's milk is actually more water efficient than almond milk is.<br /> <br /> As I've lamented before, we're at the point where we're not going to get everything we want. Fixing the world with as broken as it is, is going to come at a price.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-16T07:53:44-07:00","id":1448688,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>TheCountAlucard</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1397961-why-is-climate-change-so-controversial?p=1448556#post1448556\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><a href=\"https://youtu.be/HnJ4gjVZqao\" target=\"_blank\">Second Thought did a pretty good video</a> about water shortages.</div> </div> </div> </div>Thanks for the link. I